  "documentation": {
    "range": "<em>Pterois volitans</em>&#160;is widely distributed in the Indo-West Pacific from Cocos-Keeling, the Marquesas,&#160;and the Christmas Islands to the Pitcairn group in French Polynesia,&#160;Lord Howe Island, northern New Zealand, and the Austral Islands,&#160;and from Australia north to southern Japan and southern Korea&#160;(Schultz 1986,&#160;Allen and Erdmann 2012). It has also been introduced to the western central Atlantic, where it has spread throughout the Caribbean and Gulf of Mexico (Côté <em>et al.</em> 2013). It is typically found at depths of 1 to 50 m (Allen and Erdmann 2012), but has been recorded in the Caribbean to depths of 300 m (Albins and Hixon 2013).",
    "population": "<em>Pterois volitans </em>is common throughout its native range. After multiple aquaria releases off the coast of Florida (USA), it has successfully expanded its range into the western central Atlantic where its population is rapidly increasing (Côté <em>et al.</em> 2013).",
    "habitats": "Little information on the habitats and ecology of <em>P. volitans</em> is available from its native range, or prior to the successful invasion of the eastern Atlantic Ocean. The Red Lionfish is typically reef-associated, with regional differences in habitat plasticity in its native range (Cure <em>et al</em>. 2014). It has also been found in mangroves in the Bahamas (Barbour <em>et al</em>. 2010) and in estuarine environments in Florida (Jud <em>et al</em>. 2011). <br/><br/>The pelagic larval duration was estimated to be 20 to 35 days in the Bahamas, which is moderate to low compared to other shallow marine fishes (Ahrenholz and Morris 2010). Juveniles and adults appear to be generalists in both habitat and diet (e.g., Côté <em>et al.</em> 2013, Elise <em>et al.</em> 2015). This species is a broadcast spawner; although data are not available  from its native range, it is capable of reproducing year-round in the  western Atlantic (Morris <em>et al</em>. 2008). Size at maturity was estimated at approximately 190 mm total length, and fecundity increased rapidly with size (Gardner <em>et al.</em> 2015). It can reach a maximum length of about 38 cm.",
    "threats": "There are no known major threats to this species.",
    "measures": "There are no species specific conservation efforts in place. Its range overlaps with a number of marine protected areas&#160;(IUCN and UNEP-WCMC 2016).",
    "use_trade": "<em>Pterois volitans </em>is collected for the aquarium trade (H. Motomura pers. comm. 2015). Lionfish derbies (e.g., in the Bahamas and Florida) and fisheries have been suggested as a way to reduce the populations in its invaded range (Albins and Hixon 2013). However, the efficacy of such measures may be limited as very high mortality rates are necessary to sufficiently reduce the population (Morris <em>et al.</em> 2011).",
    "rationale": "<em>Pterois volitans </em>is widespread and common throughout its native range. It was introduced in the western Atlantic Ocean, where its population is rapidly expanded. There are no known major threats; therefore, it is listed as Least Concern.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
